course: english
lesson: 43
title: Editing and redrafting
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A first draft is like the first go at building a Lego model from your head: it stands up, but some bricks are in odd places, and editing is the second look that makes it sturdy and smart.
explain: Editing means improving the writing (a stronger word, a clearer order, a missing sentence), while proofreading means hunting the small slips: spelling, punctuation and the tense that wandered from "went" to "go".
explain: The best trick is to read your work aloud, even in a whisper: your ear trips over the gap where "the" is missing long before your eyes notice it.
explain: Spellcheck and autocorrect are helpers, not editors: "The dog bark at the mail man" passes, because every word is a real word, yet "bark" should be "barked" and the reader still stumbles.
explain: Upgrading words is good until it isn't: "He ambulated to the edifice" for "He walked to the building" sounds like a robot swallowed a dictionary; the goal is the right word, not the longest one.
fact: A draft is a version of your writing that you plan to improve.
fact: Editing means improving the content, word choice and order; proofreading means checking spelling, punctuation and grammar.
fact: Reading your work aloud helps you hear missing words, clumsy sentences and tense slips.
fact: Many schools use a special pen colour, often purple or green, for edits, so the teacher can see the improvements.
fact: Crossing out and rewriting are a normal part of writing, not a sign of failure.
fact: Professional authors usually rewrite their work many times before it is published.
fact: Spellcheck cannot catch real words in the wrong place, such as "there" for "their" or "bark" for "barked".
fact: A thesaurus gives words with similar meanings, but not every synonym fits the sentence or the reader.
fact: Peer feedback means a classmate reads your work and suggests improvements; useful feedback is kind, specific and helpful.
fact: By the end of primary school, pupils are expected to edit their own work without being told each mistake.
fact: In Manchester "boss" means great and "nowt" means nothing; in Newcastle "pet" is a friendly word like "love".
exercise: Josh says "But I already wrote it. It's done." What would help him? | A first draft can almost always be improved | Writing is only ever done once, so he is right | Editing is only for writers who got it wrong | His teacher only wants a longer story
exercise: What is the difference between editing and proofreading? | Editing improves; proofreading checks slips | Editing is done by teachers; proofreading by parents | They are two names for exactly the same job | Proofreading is for maths and editing is for English
exercise: The teacher hands out purple pens: "Purple for improvements." Why a different colour? | So the teacher can see what was changed | Because purple ink is easier to rub out later | So the page looks bright and colourful on display | Because black pens are only for spelling tests
exercise: Ruby reads her work aloud, whispering, and stops: "Oh! I missed 'the'." Why did reading aloud help? | Her ear caught a gap her eyes skipped | Whispering makes spelling correct by magic | It made the story longer and better | Teachers give marks for whispering
exercise: Kai from Manchester says "Me mam says me writing's boss, so I'm not changing nowt." What does "nowt" mean? | Nothing | Now | Not yet | Nobody
exercise: What is the best reply to Kai? | Praise is great, but every writer can improve | Your mam is wrong and your writing is rubbish | You must change every single word in it | Never show your mam your writing again
exercise: Year 6: "Check your tenses." Ava finds "I go" in her past-tense story. What should it become? | I went | I was go | I will go | I going
exercise: Freya worries "What if they're mean about my work?" during swap-and-feedback. What makes feedback useful? | Kind, specific comments with one thing to improve | Only ever saying "it's rubbish" so that the point sinks in | Never saying anything good at all, only the bad things | Copying the other person's story
exercise: Ella says "Autocorrect fixed everything." Her story says "The dog bark at the mail man." What did it miss? | "bark" should be "barked" | "dog" should be spelled "dogg" | "the" should be spelled "teh" | Nothing, it is perfect as it is
exercise: Why did autocorrect miss it? | "Bark" is a real word, just the wrong form | Autocorrect only checks the first word of each sentence | Autocorrect is switched off on Thursdays | Dogs are not in its dictionary
exercise: Leo upgrades every verb: "He ambulated to the edifice." What went wrong? | The words are too fancy and unclear | He used too few verbs in the sentence | He should have used even longer words than those | "Ambulated" is spelled wrongly
exercise: Grandad in Newcastle says, dry: "In my day we wrote it right first time, pet." What is true? | Most professional writers rewrite many times | Good writers never need to change anything they have written | Only children need to edit their work | Writers in the past never made mistakes
exercise: Why does the Year 6 teacher need evidence that children edit on their own? | Editing alone is a key skill by the end of primary | Teachers are too busy to read any of the stories | It is the only thing that is marked in Year 6 | Children are not allowed to get help any more
exercise: Which is a proofreading job? | Correcting "thier" to "their" | Adding a new and exciting ending | Changing who the main character is | Moving a paragraph to the start
exercise: Which is an editing job? | Swapping a dull opening for a hook | Adding a missing full stop | Correcting a spelling of "because" | Putting a capital on "london"
exercise: What does crossing out show in a draft? | That the writer is thinking and improving | That the writer has completely failed the task they were set | That the pen has run out of ink | That the work must be thrown away
test: Your mate goes "editing is for people who mess up, I'm too good for it." What is the truth? | Even great writers edit; it's part of writing | Good writers never need a second look at their work, ever | Editing means your writing was bad | Only teachers are allowed to edit
test: ★★★ Spellcheck finds no errors in "Their going too the park to sea there friends." How many words are wrong? | Four | None | Two | Six
test: In a cartoon, a robot "improves" a story by replacing every word with the longest synonym. What happens? | The story becomes harder to read | The story becomes much clearer and simpler for everyone | The story turns into a poem | Nothing changes at all
test: What would happen if Ruby never read her work aloud? | She might miss small gaps and slips | Her spelling would improve on its own | Her stories would always come out perfect | Teachers would stop reading them at all
test: A Gen Z sister says "Editing your story? That's lowkey nerdy." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) What is the best reply? | Every real author edits | Yes, only nerds need to look at their work twice | Editing is banned in secondary school | Authors never edit their books
test: A lad from Glasgow, bit gallus ("gallus" means cocky), says "Ma first draft's perfect, nae changes." What would a good editor ask? | Have you read it aloud to check? | Why did you write it at all? | Can you make it shorter by half? | Why not use a different pen?
test: ★★★ Which change is an edit, not a proofread? | Adding a sentence showing a feeling | Changing "dont" to "don't" | Adding a full stop at the end | Changing "i" to "I"
test: Your partner writes on your story: "It's bad." Why is it not useful? | It doesn't say what or how to improve | It is far too kind and much too detailed to read | It uses too many words | It is written in pen
test: ★★★ A story says: "I runned to the shop and buyed some sweets, then I eat them all." How many verbs need fixing? | Three | One | Two | Five
test: A kid in Sydney says "My story's sweet as, no worries." ("Sweet as" means great.) What should she still do? | Read it through once more to check | Throw it away and start again | Hand it in without looking | Add "sweet as" to every line
test: Which tool can tell you that "there" should be "their"? | Careful reading for meaning | Only the spellcheck on a laptop | A thesaurus | A ruler
exam: Identify the activity that constitutes proofreading. | Correction of spelling and punctuation errors | The invention of a new plot for the narrative | The replacement of the protagonist | The addition of a further chapter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of professional authors? | They customarily revise their work on numerous occasions | They invariably compose a perfect first draft | They never permit others to read their drafts | They submit their work without reading it
exam: Select the principal limitation of automated spelling correction. | It misses correctly spelt words used in error | It alters every word that is longer than five letters | It functions only in the morning | It cannot recognise any English words
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate response to feedback received from a classmate. | Consider it carefully and use what improves the work | Reject it immediately in all circumstances | Copy the classmate's work in its entirety | Request that the classmate cease reading
